# What must never be uploaded to a build.
#
# `vercel deploy` from the CLI uploads the working tree, and it reads THIS file
# rather than .gitignore. Without it, a local `.env` — which on a developer
# machine holds a live inference key and the recorder keypair path — would be
# packed into the deployment, and a local `dist/` would mask the clean-checkout
# build failure that `docs/deployment.md` describes.
#
# A Git-connected deploy does not use this file; it builds from the commit, so
# gitignored files are absent anyway. This protects the CLI path specifically.

# Credentials. The first line is the one that matters.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Built output: the build must produce this from source, on Vercel, from a
# clean checkout. Shipping a local copy hides a broken build until the next
# person clones the repo.
dist/
.next/
out/
target/
node_modules/

# Local caches. These are the ones that actually bite: .turbo/cache alone is
# ~700MB here and holds individual archives past Vercel's 100MB per-file
# limit, so a deploy fails on upload rather than on anything to do with the
# code. All of them are gitignored, which is exactly why they need naming
# here — this file is read instead of .gitignore, not as well as it.
.turbo/
.vercel/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Not part of the web build.
.git/
infra/
docs/
scripts/
programs/
backend/api/src/
backend/api/tests/
backend/api/scripts/
.github/
*.log
